You’re right that absolute certainty is rare. We usually can’t inspect a memory and prove its origin from the feeling alone. In that sense, our confidence is probabilistic.

But probability doesn’t mean all possibilities are equally likely. Evidence can make one explanation much better supported: a photo taken at the time, a note written before the memory was discussed, or independent people recalling the same detail all raise confidence. A detail that first appears after seeing an edited image or hearing someone else’s account deserves more caution.

Even those checks aren’t perfect. Photos can be altered; notes can be mistaken; people can influence one another. So the honest answer is often not “I know with certainty,” but “this is strongly supported,” “this is plausible,” or “I’m not sure.”

The practical skill is calibration: matching confidence to the evidence, and staying open to revision. We can’t always get certainty, but we can still tell the difference between a guess, a supported memory, and a fact independently checked.